{"id":"nursing-home-related-money","title":"Nursing homes whose assets are mostly money owed by related companies","question":"How much of what a nursing home owns is a balance owed to it by its parent, home office, owner or other related companies, and how has that grown beside its income?","finding":"42 of 183 Bay Area nursing homes with a recent State financial report (40 of them investor-owned) record at least half of all their assets, and at least $3 million, as money owed to them by related parties: $1,021.5 million in all. Largest against its size among investor-owned homes: Garfield Neurobehavioral Center, Oakland, with $33.6 million owed to it in 2025, 87% of its assets and 3.3 times a year's revenue. 3 are in San Francisco ($63.4 million): San Francisco Post Acute, Victorian Post Acute, Central Gardens Post Acute. Across investor-owned Bay Area homes, the amount owed by related parties went from $900.4 million in 2018 to $958.4 million in 2025; the same homes reported $1,124.7 million of net income over those years.","how":"From each home's yearly financial report to the State health-data department (selected file, report years 2018 to 2025; Bay Area extract, skilled nursing licences only). \"Owed by related parties\" is the sum of the report's current and non-current related-party receivables, less related-party payables; the State defines the first as amounts \"due from the facility's parent, home office, owner or other related parties\". A few homes book the balance as a negative amount owed; it is counted as owed to them and added back to assets so the share is comparable. A home is listed when the net amount is at least half its total assets and at least $3 million in its latest report. Administration and lease expense are divided by resident days; \"times the Bay Area middle\" compares administration per day with the median home that year. Revenue per Medi-Cal and Medicare day is net of contractual adjustments and includes managed-care plans. Chain is the affiliated entity in the federal enrollment file, reached through the State facility crosswalk.","limits":"A balance owed by a related company is not a payment and not a loss. The usual reason is central cash management: the chain's home office collects each home's cash and pays its bills, and the home books what it is owed. Not-for-profit senior communities pool money across campuses in the same way. The balance can be repaid, and a home may owe related parties money on other lines. The State file has no line for management fees or for rent paid to a related landlord: fees sit inside administration expense and rent inside lease expense, with unrelated costs, so those columns only show which homes run high. The federal cost-report file on disk has no related-party worksheet at all. Reports for 2023 to 2025 are as submitted, not yet audited by the State; report periods can be shorter than a year; and a few reports contain obvious slips, which are noted and left out. Related companies each home named in 2020 are listed on the related-company page built from the State list.","tier":"REDFLAG","columns":[{"k":"home","t":"Nursing home","type":"org"},{"k":"city","t":"City","type":"text"},{"k":"control","t":"Type","type":"text"},{"k":"chain","t":"Chain (federal file)","type":"text"},{"k":"year","t":"Report year","type":"year"},{"k":"relatedNet","t":"Owed to it by related parties, net","type":"usd"},{"k":"shareOfAssets","t":"Share of all assets","type":"pct"},{"k":"timesRevenue","t":"Against a year's revenue","type":"times"},{"k":"firstYear","t":"Earliest report","type":"year"},{"k":"relatedNetFirst","t":"Owed to it then","type":"usd"},{"k":"netIncome","t":"Net income, latest year","type":"usd"},{"k":"netIncomeSince","t":"Net income, all years on file","type":"usd"},{"k":"adminPerDay","t":"Administration per resident day","type":"usd"},{"k":"adminTimes","t":"Against the Bay Area middle","type":"times"},{"k":"leasePerDay","t":"Lease and rent per resident day","type":"usd"},{"k":"revPerDay","t":"Revenue per resident day","type":"usd"},{"k":"mediCalPerDay","t":"Revenue per Medi-Cal day","type":"usd"},{"k":"medicarePerDay","t":"Revenue per Medicare day","type":"usd"},{"k":"note","t":"Note","type":"text"}],"rows":[{"home":"Garfield Neurobehavioral Center","city":"Oakland","county":"Alameda","control":"Investor Owned","chain":"Telecare Corporation (parent named in the State report)","year":2025,"relatedNet":33601960,"shareOfAssets":0.87,"timesRevenue":3.32,"firstYear":2018,"relatedNetFirst":20286445,"netIncome":1738946,"netIncomeSince":14719688,"adminPerDay":99,"adminTimes":0.8,"leasePerDay":1,"revPerDay":469,"mediCalPerDay":469,"medicarePerDay":null,"note":null},{"home":"Plum Tree Care Center","city":"San Jose","county":"Santa Clara","control":"Investor Owned","chain":"Generations Healthcare","year":2025,"relatedNet":43235904,"shareOfAssets":0.812,"timesRevenue":2.31,"firstYear":2018,"relatedNetFirst":23894561,"netIncome":3252097,"netIncomeSince":14991226,"adminPerDay":137,"adminTimes":1.11,"leasePerDay":25,"revPerDay":789,"mediCalPerDay":435,"medicarePerDay":1126,"note":null},{"home":"McClure Post Acute","city":"Oakland","county":"Alameda","control":"Investor Owned","chain":"PACS Group","year":2025,"relatedNet":33344946,"shareOfAssets":0.931,"timesRevenue":1.99,"firstYear":2018,"relatedNetFirst":2947717,"netIncome":3832900,"netIncomeSince":31824139,"adminPerDay":170,"adminTimes":1.37,"leasePerDay":42,"revPerDay":826,"mediCalPerDay":399,"medicarePerDay":1133,"note":null},{"home":"Cedar Crest Nursing and Rehab Center","city":"Sunnyvale","county":"Santa Clara","control":"Investor Owned","chain":"Generations Healthcare","year":2025,"relatedNet":45605438,"shareOfAssets":0.804,"timesRevenue":1.99,"firstYear":2018,"relatedNetFirst":21884444,"netIncome":5075095,"netIncomeSince":21722340,"adminPerDay":127,"adminTimes":1.03,"leasePerDay":33,"revPerDay":746,"mediCalPerDay":375,"medicarePerDay":1066,"note":null},{"home":"Vista Manor Nursing Cent